-- What evaluation does with an input nobody supplied, once it can carry
-- one along instead of stopping at it (UNKNOWN-EVALUATION-SPEC §8 step 3).
--
-- Every input in the section below is unsupplied. A connective whose known
-- operand decides it answers whatever the unknown is (`x AND FALSE` is
-- `FALSE`); a comparison, an arithmetic operation, a field of an unknown
-- record and a call to an assumed function become terms that name the input;
-- and a directive whose answer still depends on an input is undetermined,
-- which prints "I needed to know the value of …" and names every input it
-- waits on, not only the first. The row numbers below are §4.12's.
--
-- `l4 run` EXITS 1 ON THIS FILE, BY DESIGN: an undetermined directive fails
-- the run, as a stuck one always has (§4.7.4).

IMPORT prelude

DECLARE Person HAS age IS A NUMBER

ASSUME g IS A FUNCTION FROM NUMBER TO BOOLEAN

GIVEN k IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
older k MEANS age GREATER THAN k

GIVEN b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`true or` b MEANS TRUE OR b

GIVEN b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`or true` b MEANS b OR TRUE

GIVEN b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`and an error` b MEANS b AND (1 DIVIDED BY 0 GREATER THAN 0)

DECIDE total IS 1 PLUS 2 PLUS 3

-- About 540,000 machine steps: more than the step limit (250,000) allows once
-- the counter has started. Called afresh in each directive, never cached.
GIVEN i IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`count up from` i MEANS IF i AT LEAST 20000 THEN i ELSE `count up from` (i PLUS 1)

§ `Unknown inputs`
    GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
          y IS A BOOLEAN
          n IS A NUMBER
          age IS A NUMBER
          d IS A Person
          m IS A MAYBE NUMBER

-- A known operand that decides the connective decides it (rows 1 to 3, 5).
#EVAL x AND FALSE
#EVAL x OR TRUE
#EVAL x UNLESS TRUE
#EVAL and (LIST TRUE, x, FALSE)

-- One that does not leaves the unknown, and the directive is undetermined,
-- naming it (rows 4 and 6; `IMPLIES` keeps its node whatever its right
-- operand is, so even `x IMPLIES TRUE` waits on `x` until build step 4).
#EVAL x IMPLIES TRUE
#EVALTRACE NOT x
#EVAL TRUE AND (x IMPLIES TRUE)
#EVAL (x IMPLIES TRUE) AND (y IMPLIES TRUE)

-- Two unknowns: the answer names both (rows 23 and 24).
#EVAL x AND y
#ASSERT x AND y
#ASSERT NOT (x AND y)

-- An unknown equals itself (rows 10, 42, 66), and an equality over BOOLEANs
-- is the biconditional, so `x EQUALS TRUE` is `x`.
#EVAL x EQUALS x
#EVAL n PLUS 1 EQUALS n PLUS 1
#EVAL d's age EQUALS d's age
#EVAL x EQUALS TRUE

-- Comparisons are atoms, keyed by their term, and so is arithmetic over an
-- unknown (rows 11, 12, 13, 15, 44, 46). The traces print the residual.
#EVALTRACE n EQUALS 3
#EVALTRACE 3 EQUALS n
#EVALTRACE older 18 AND NOT older 65
#EVAL age AT LEAST 18 OR age LESS THAN 18
#EVAL n PLUS 1
#EVALTRACE elem n (LIST 1, 2)

-- A field of an unknown record names the field path (rows 35, 37).
#EVAL d's age
#EVAL d's age AT LEAST 18

-- An assumed function applied (rows 39, 40, 45).
#EVAL g 3 AND NOT g 4
#EVAL FALSE AND g 3
#EVAL elem g (LIST g)

-- Lists and calls whose body decides anyway (rows 47 to 50, 69).
#EVAL elem 3 (LIST 3, n)
#EVAL elem 3 (LIST n, 3)
#EVAL `true or` x
#EVAL `or true` x
#EVAL FALSE AND `and an error` TRUE

-- Unchanged: a known left operand behaves as it always did (rows 17, 18).
#EVAL FALSE AND (1 DIVIDED BY 0 GREATER THAN 0)
#EVAL (1 DIVIDED BY 0 GREATER THAN 0) AND FALSE

-- A refusal (rows 75 to 77): reached under no unknown it is an answer;
-- reached only under an unknown left operand it is undetermined, naming the
-- unknown, and never a refusal, until build step 5 builds guarded leaves.
#EVAL TBD AND x
#EVAL FALSE AND TBD
#EVAL x AND TBD
-- An error there is the same (U11's interim).
#EVAL x AND (1 DIVIDED BY 0 GREATER THAN 0)
-- And so is running out of steps there (§4.5): with no limit this would be
-- `x OR TRUE`, which is TRUE; past the limit it gives up, naming `x`.
#EVAL x OR (`count up from` 0 EQUALS 20000)

-- A CONSIDER, IF, coercion or JSONENCODE on an unknown is undetermined,
-- naming what it waits on, and never takes a catch-all (rows 34, 70, 83,
-- 84, 85).
#EVAL CONSIDER (x AND y) WHEN TRUE THEN 1, OTHERWISE 2
#EVAL CONSIDER m WHEN NOTHING THEN 1, WHEN JUST z THEN 2
#EVAL CONSIDER m WHEN JUST z THEN 2, OTHERWISE 1
#EVAL IF n GREATER THAN 3 THEN 1 ELSE 2
#EVAL n AS STRING
#EVAL JSONENCODE n

-- A result whose only unknowns are bare inputs is the value it is (rows 60
-- and 67); one that holds any other term is undetermined. The second is
-- longer than the step limit, and still prints its value: nothing here
-- reached a site with a term, so the counter never started (C4).
#EVAL LIST n, total
#EVAL LIST n, `count up from` 0
#EVAL LIST x, (y OR TRUE)
#EVAL LIST n, (n PLUS 1)
#EVAL JUST (x AND y)
